answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "784ae653-35b6-5020-84f0-16e9723eee86" :question/math-academy-id "q-158193" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined for all points $(x,y)$ on the plane, hence the domain is the entire $xy$-plane. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/402d90507403eabf4cd35cde83a45c9fa2d8080347e1debb0e2463be408b489b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4fd256-a726-523c-84dc-f62d4e2711cc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Domain of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89adea9c-2624-51f5-87be-d146f924f793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1899/Source/Images/q-158195-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121328} {:answer/id #uuid "994f7808-cf0f-56bc-a8ea-c3a948dc2e0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1899/Source/Images/q-158195-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121329} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e490c9-4c0f-58b0-a9e3-11ae84168e19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1899/Source/Images/q-158195-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121330} {:answer/id #uuid "01bc999d-a393-5ca2-a72a-0e0326d9b709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1899/Source/Images/q-158195-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121331} {:answer/id #uuid "7c5f87de-f15b-545d-a104-14554375362c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1899/Source/Images/q-158195-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89adea9c-2624-51f5-87be-d146f924f793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1899/Source/Images/q-158195-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121328} :answer-field/id #uuid "47471bd5-5fe6-5bd0-9539-623b2f234c35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76db17ba-163a-5fe3-9bd7-63e519e3eab2" :question/math-academy-id "q-158195" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $\\ge $ sign, the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ is part of the domain. Therefore, we draw a solid curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and shade the area *outside* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/ba31bbc16c58860a4bc3c4d33ea1a61cb3d86d0e73d8aa6a67869b3e96a7fe32.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4fd256-a726-523c-84dc-f62d4e2711cc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Domain of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7d23f53-9357-5c17-9aa7-42c0932712f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/3070e936fea02f7c02b2e6bc780534b80108db28836c4c4615954e8063590867.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313957} {:answer/id #uuid "fedaeb7f-5c45-53c5-ba74-14aa0d51306a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/b49f91c2047e8ce566bc50febf7a3e445c26938a3869247ad1dc3ff5a60215ed.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313958} {:answer/id #uuid "1618107a-64e8-5737-980c-d2487890a6b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/45c170dbe0048816d6490ff39c6144d3ac42298dff9c2fa71cb22217d358ce1a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313959} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ca991d-c26e-59a8-b884-a792b6b60e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/2ddf4edfd91b14835e7b14ca8cebf08b82b8585d9fd18972c8b065d6153a62f0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313960} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5c8907-2ec9-52df-81ca-b8f34c4e29f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/f04a668ae876db9e3a26f8ce7cc8bfcaac1149ee221622c22343483fd89f7f44.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fedaeb7f-5c45-53c5-ba74-14aa0d51306a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/b49f91c2047e8ce566bc50febf7a3e445c26938a3869247ad1dc3ff5a60215ed.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313958} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e1c9478-84c6-5395-b1dd-c133f9ff2291" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c95700f-899d-582f-9a0f-aa45401ba91e" :question/math-academy-id "q-158198" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{y}^{2}\\le {x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,-|x|\\le y\\le |x|\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-|x|\\le y\\le |x|}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $\\le $ sign, the curves are part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the solid curves $y=-|x|$ and $y=|x|,$ and shade the area *below* the curve $y=|x|,$ and *above* the curve $y=-|x|.$\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/95f187d2857973e4fea83eedf47bd54c153ce2a77f278cfb992b222de2d91ab8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313962 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4fd256-a726-523c-84dc-f62d4e2711cc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Domain of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdeba301-b61c-51b3-9ced-b6c0e2a52d41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/f843c386c730f85e7c3511e4fd8631b01f77b998f4e99fe19ed9783e4c4b6cbb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313964} {:answer/id #uuid "181f45c0-4433-54cd-bf9e-0846e5d65d16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/89076b709a935f384acab2609f2d046123ceb3984c5ae60818f908525ad5e53d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313965} {:answer/id #uuid "56033afe-6cd8-536c-8e8a-629b214a7c55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/4030364abd4fd5a8f21e4ac358adb077ed56fb2a33f14ed49d4131e7ab36af6d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313966} {:answer/id #uuid "f5bec430-6f7d-5a67-9401-414700b7d366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/43693fc51d09f6408198a74dc5672a2904b1ad0d45f6abc7f1f4aad0b2764a5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313967} {:answer/id #uuid "45916c85-8031-52cd-8597-288f8e78ecc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/44e6511542b39bb3946095ff28c7a82ab3e35b5e50d648aba246413e6fea00c4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "181f45c0-4433-54cd-bf9e-0846e5d65d16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/89076b709a935f384acab2609f2d046123ceb3984c5ae60818f908525ad5e53d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313965} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e7aa36b-52f9-5c28-841b-20c83d4f7657" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa9a90c8-68ec-5392-a3c8-0ae9ab63850c" :question/math-academy-id "q-158202" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\ny-2x>0\\,⟹\\,y>2x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>2x}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $>$ sign, the line is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed line $y=2x$ and shade the region *above* the line.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/42e2faf3df63b038b57d2fddc9b7ef2c2e720dae6e0a8524d602b7ac93681b3d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4fd256-a726-523c-84dc-f62d4e2711cc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Domain of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc4bead9-1005-55a7-b7fc-146c18b16afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/c30be01de07727a44fce390cfc2c56ddeed8071a7a7d64abec831a31aa2079fe.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313971} {:answer/id #uuid "48269c1a-7750-59e2-b4e2-084488c18e62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/402d90507403eabf4cd35cde83a45c9fa2d8080347e1debb0e2463be408b489b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313972} {:answer/id #uuid "e2063e47-5b0d-5c9f-8da9-c90d81bc426d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/fefbca84defd52b9fecd3b13b37cb95a744b4ddedd5a570ad554b6a251cd6077.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313973} {:answer/id #uuid "c18b88bb-6a0b-5ea3-9a04-9351dfb5fd03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/4789f1e3a4fbe0c6f23980010a0222905cb46b4ef105b02e7fba0b59fc570eeb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313974} {:answer/id #uuid "002008dd-d1e2-5892-b583-d4943905ff76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/65e29ff9fda5ad25fbbd68121d0c1a86e27a41de8890af31425208d7c38f2c2f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2063e47-5b0d-5c9f-8da9-c90d81bc426d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/fefbca84defd52b9fecd3b13b37cb95a744b4ddedd5a570ad554b6a251cd6077.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313973} :answer-field/id #uuid "82ca35ef-502b-5e19-96e4-42fcce7e59a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9419d3b0-0595-5357-8a67-2ff487c67499" :question/math-academy-id "q-158205" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,(x.y)\\ne (0,0)}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $\\ne $ sign, the point $(0,0)$ is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we exclude the point $(x,y)=(0,0)$ and shade the plane.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041908/assets/701c54bff1c431883f21cb980ac43f4f2f6beabb11817066a4e909d5a2663a25.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e593685-f5ca-5501-94bf-7cb4d49e935b" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Involving Reciprocals and Radicals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddfd747f-06f9-5221-b1cc-bbdf384c3bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,2x+y>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313978} {:answer/id #uuid "d79a226c-ea71-5391-8ab0-d5dfdb1928e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,2x+y\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313979} {:answer/id #uuid "d958d60d-6245-5dc4-9c20-90d3eb2194ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy-$plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313980} {:answer/id #uuid "14cb20f7-0554-5b7d-b721-ba89cd624c71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,2x+y<1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313981} {:answer/id #uuid "90aa2d13-5943-5a4e-9f2c-f8962b4ac59d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,2x+y\\ne -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d79a226c-ea71-5391-8ab0-d5dfdb1928e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,2x+y\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313979} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeb808fd-556f-58c5-8572-702c5af657c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65cbd85f-b582-52c8-8697-6b2f875bc91b" :question/math-academy-id "q-158553" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2x+y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n2x+y\\ne 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,2x+y\\ne 0}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e593685-f5ca-5501-94bf-7cb4d49e935b" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Involving Reciprocals and Radicals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4e48201-1bc2-5f1a-b97d-64b2b42f3a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy-$plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313985} {:answer/id #uuid "d4447f86-89a9-5dd8-972e-b294b8fb5316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313986} {:answer/id #uuid "29a7164f-d352-511b-a303-b58dfeb81683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313987} {:answer/id #uuid "855eb4e1-95e2-58e1-8579-afc0fcf89186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313988} {:answer/id #uuid "bc6a14e6-83f6-59ee-8e06-20eb316c38a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "855eb4e1-95e2-58e1-8579-afc0fcf89186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313988} :answer-field/id #uuid "efe920b9-7db8-57a3-8644-7592ecccb77c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6268386-e99d-5e4d-9d89-793792baff62" :question/math-academy-id "q-158556" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e593685-f5ca-5501-94bf-7cb4d49e935b" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Involving Reciprocals and Radicals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af86311d-93ea-5b88-9e9b-ea3bc1d4774a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x<1,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313992} {:answer/id #uuid "76cd2a90-81b2-5c9d-b4dc-9bd9a5880dae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy-$plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313993} {:answer/id #uuid "60980cef-ffe0-5b9e-bd5a-d0a5658a7d77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ge 1,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313994} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e22289-69af-5301-bf60-f710f207d5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ge 1,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313995} {:answer/id #uuid "a907bc54-1ea3-5a8a-9535-37738a9081f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x=1,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60980cef-ffe0-5b9e-bd5a-d0a5658a7d77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ge 1,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313994} :answer-field/id #uuid "4833dbb9-2e77-5d1d-aa06-8c10302d1c7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7febc1cf-0efe-5a67-b174-b4b58e108a7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-158558" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{\\sqrt{x-1}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-1\\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x\\ge 1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ge 1,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f2bc84-03e8-5921-81e2-683a09195279" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Containing Exponential, Logarithmic, and Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7289041-b326-5be9-a41d-6545a92bf3c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313999} {:answer/id #uuid "245ee1ed-1c13-5e70-8e9c-020338484221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314000} {:answer/id #uuid "91a46a93-6bb0-530f-acf4-91e666c761d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314001} {:answer/id #uuid "9f27ac63-d5c6-5be6-8928-283ec65b3daa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314002} {:answer/id #uuid "804ef20b-9399-58df-915d-e322e3101a59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91a46a93-6bb0-530f-acf4-91e666c761d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314001} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec424ffb-5f1d-55b2-a20c-70b61fb12454" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1029a828-f598-5659-b5f7-4a32ed5e8c7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-158567" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}/y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}$ is defined for $x$ and $y,$ such that $y\\ne 0,$ and\n\n- ${e}^{g(x,y)}$ is defined if $g(x,y)$ is defined.\n\nAs a result, ${e}^{{x}^{2}/y}$ is defined for values of $x$ and $y$ that satisfy $y\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9289c4d-4f7f-5704-a2cb-272f92d8fef3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84903e8a-04c0-5812-8b26-fa7bfaa50e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314006} {:answer/id #uuid "755de695-5f32-5041-8052-af430d737c73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314007} {:answer/id #uuid "cdad8d55-0ad0-5baa-92f0-df1f8ef1b077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314008} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c6cafb-62a8-57e0-8514-92a1418328b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314009} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed64059-a061-5882-9e99-1a2afe0d4377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdad8d55-0ad0-5baa-92f0-df1f8ef1b077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314008} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecc9f56f-3c4b-5e4c-8a21-5fa60d1cab2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04d132d6-8bc5-5f6e-9e90-1955dd5825ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-158572" :question/problem "Evaluate $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-2y+4)$ at the point $(-4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(-4,1),$ we substitute $x=-4$ and $y=1$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{|}_{(-4,1)} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-2y+4)\\,{|}_{(-4,1)} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3(-4)-2(1)+4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-12-2+4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-10),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f2bc84-03e8-5921-81e2-683a09195279" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Containing Exponential, Logarithmic, and Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52c88192-440f-50ae-9291-f67d503697be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314013} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d51484-df5e-55d7-8d3f-eb2c5f1f7d15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314014} {:answer/id #uuid "0e83191c-ca11-5e70-b8e8-8f28a90f49f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314015} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4d6249-ed32-5732-a264-9a42c9c5daf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314016} {:answer/id #uuid "3581c2de-980c-5f28-8ac8-a24a5f4a515b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e83191c-ca11-5e70-b8e8-8f28a90f49f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314015} :answer-field/id #uuid "da0e463e-b68a-5e97-abf9-a0aee0d1f195" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28bbe35f-2865-53c1-8aa2-7dcb52e770e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-158573" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is defined for all $x$ and $y,$ and\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡g(x,y)$ is defined if $g(x,y)$ is defined.\n\nAs a result, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined for all values of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the domain of $f$ is the entire $xy$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314018 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9289c4d-4f7f-5704-a2cb-272f92d8fef3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40db3f21-42ef-5d83-b5c8-dc3aaaf9bd70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314020} {:answer/id #uuid "dde6fbfd-41aa-5970-a2f3-765e9e12aaf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314021} {:answer/id #uuid "60ee74c5-5921-50fd-9770-ea57a242d647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314022} {:answer/id #uuid "af23ff20-ada2-5d88-adfd-d37e3dcba6e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314023} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5a44f5-0aa0-5f67-ad46-d0de39c54f61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c5a44f5-0aa0-5f67-ad46-d0de39c54f61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314024} :answer-field/id #uuid "240c091b-d56a-52e5-a8a4-945940b06fe9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53164591-e14a-51c2-9c43-d71e8a660ae9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158574" :question/problem "Evaluate $z=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-1$ at the point $(3,{e}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(3,{e}^{2}),$ we substitute $x=3$ and $y={e}^{2}$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{|}_{(3,{e}^{2})} & =(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-1){|}_{(3,{e}^{2})} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})-1 \\\\  & =3(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)-1 \\\\  & =3(2)-1 \\\\  & =6-1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f2bc84-03e8-5921-81e2-683a09195279" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Containing Exponential, Logarithmic, and Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78526ec9-0439-5085-acdb-d21d5e5c8ebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314027} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e80373-51b6-5192-8198-1246a6b3745b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0,\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314028} {:answer/id #uuid "e725e7b2-393f-515a-9fa0-5514c05f63cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314029} {:answer/id #uuid "cc872385-1917-5e68-bf20-234544c898a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 0,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314030} {:answer/id #uuid "f63aa09d-2173-570d-946a-434a0eaba68c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1e80373-51b6-5192-8198-1246a6b3745b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0,\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314028} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ec68fbd-b062-593e-9259-ec1ea26aa728" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea20951b-22e3-530a-96bb-bc43df7bfe3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-158577" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0,\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9289c4d-4f7f-5704-a2cb-272f92d8fef3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97593fa0-06b9-5ccf-9823-d3f4b743dbfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314034} {:answer/id #uuid "1d4f40eb-999b-56d6-926e-a43c332c1694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314035} {:answer/id #uuid "218be841-6c83-5e49-8279-9ff7b83ec28c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314036} {:answer/id #uuid "dbaf9ba8-2564-5510-8694-99ebdd1c8ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314037} {:answer/id #uuid "35ae9d4f-0aa0-59b2-87ae-f1818766ff16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35ae9d4f-0aa0-59b2-87ae-f1818766ff16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314038} :answer-field/id #uuid "1beeecfd-e204-56c9-ac3a-e85167429293" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43b2f787-cce3-5cd1-8de1-58a916bc4b85" :question/math-academy-id "q-158588" :question/problem "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined as \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3{y}^{3}-x+2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate this function at the point $(5,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(5,-1),$ we substitute $x=5$ and $y=-1$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5,-1) & =(3{y}^{3}-x+2){|}_{(5,-1)} \\\\  & =3(-1)^{3}-5+2 \\\\  & =-3-5+2 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9289c4d-4f7f-5704-a2cb-272f92d8fef3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "faa54ec7-55cd-5efb-a2ed-e32367f6829e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314041} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3413fa-2b03-5839-80de-34a40207932f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314042} {:answer/id #uuid "09b49afc-af85-5124-8fe2-5f3c87f4c984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314043} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba8b777-8117-5816-b392-e914e2b237fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314044} {:answer/id #uuid "9be51a1b-7a40-571a-8196-1abaf4656670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt[3]{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a3413fa-2b03-5839-80de-34a40207932f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314042} :answer-field/id #uuid "5305ad78-ce55-59f8-8efc-a9fa974af492" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "055ae06f-606c-5e3b-8cdc-232b1da51ea5" :question/math-academy-id "q-158591" :question/problem "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined as \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{xy+7}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate this function at the point $(-4,-5).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(-4,-5),$ we substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-5$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4,-5) & =\\sqrt[3]{xy+7}\\,{|}_{(-4,-5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{(-4)(-5)+7} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{20+7} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314046 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9289c4d-4f7f-5704-a2cb-272f92d8fef3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12755720-e6ee-57b4-a933-fca3a3ea96fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314048} {:answer/id #uuid "9c27daa2-1b16-5c47-a164-2c91e03df527" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314049} {:answer/id #uuid "b91a23e5-314f-5697-aaf2-b2c1983ebe9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314050} {:answer/id #uuid "f2fef044-4224-5e36-867e-4b811c5bb450" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314051} {:answer/id #uuid "1534c771-80a3-53e2-8558-59ddbcab59b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2fef044-4224-5e36-867e-4b811c5bb450" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314051} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b74f5ce-9071-5f91-bad0-4e7176a2eef1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f42f62f-58b6-5f9d-92cf-bf829376330c" :question/math-academy-id "q-158592" :question/problem "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined as \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nEvaluate this function at the point $(-6,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(-6,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ we substitute $x=-6$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-6,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,{|}_{(-6,-\\pi /4)} \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-6(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
